<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Landau</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Landau in der Pfalz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Landau in der Pfalz is a town in the Palatinate, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It became a free imperial city in 1291 and a member of the Decapolis of Alsatian towns in 1521. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> held it from the late 17th century, when Vauban made it a fortress, until 1815; it then passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. It is now a university town in the wine country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/landau.jpg" alt="Landau" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BertholdD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Court_House_in_Landau.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Landau in der Pfalz is an autonomous town surrounded by the Südliche Weinstraße district of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>. It lies east of the Palatinate Forest, on the German Wine Route, among vineyards and wine-growing villages, and wine-making continues to be an important industry. It has been a university town since 1990. The districts of Arzheim, Dammheim, Godramstein, Mörlheim, Mörzheim, Nussdorf, Queichheim and Wollmesheim belong to it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Landau was first mentioned as a settlement in 1106 and was held by the counts of Leiningen-Dagsburg-Landeck. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf I of Germany</a> granted it a charter in 1274 and declared it a Free Imperial Town in 1291. Prince-Bishop Emich of Speyer, a major landowner in the district, seized the town in 1324, and it regained its ancient rights only in 1511, from Maximilian I. An Augustinian monastery was founded there in 1276.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 control of Landau was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, with certain ill-defined reservations, and the town was part of France from 1680 to 1815. Vauban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s military architect, gave it its modern fortifications in 1688-1699, making the little town, of about 5,000 inhabitants in 1789, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s strongest citadels.</p><p>It underwent four sieges in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. The French lost it in 1702, regained it in 1703 after the Battle of Speyerbach, lost it again in a siege that ended on 23 November 1704, and recaptured it in a final siege from 6 June to 20 August 1713 under Marshal Villars. During the 1704 siege King Joseph I arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in a newly developed convertible carriage, which became popular and was named the landau.</p><p>Landau remained French after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s Hundred Days and was granted to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1815, becoming the seat of one of the thirteen Bezirksämter of the Bavarian Rheinkreis, later renamed Pfalz. The political cartoonist Thomas Nast was born there in 1840. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it was an important barracks town for the French occupation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The large main square, the Rathausplatz, is dominated by the town hall and the market hall, the Altes Kaufhaus. The Protestant Collegiate Church, begun in the 14th century and completed in the mid-16th, is one of the oldest buildings. The Festhalle, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a> style, dates from 1905-1907. The former fortifications gave way in the 19th century to a ring road around the old town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Landau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The league maintained federal fortresses at Mainz, Luxembourg and <b>Landau</b>, and after the Rhine Crisis of 1840 with France it built new ones at Ulm and Rastatt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Mulhouse left in 1515 to ally with the Swiss and <b>Landau</b> joined in 1521.</blockquote><a href="/state/decapole">Décapole</a></li><li><blockquote>The members divided over the Reformation, with Colmar and <b>Landau</b> becoming Protestant while others stayed Catholic.</blockquote><a href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a></li><li><blockquote>Founded by the counts of Leiningen around 1260, <b>Landau</b> received town rights from King Rudolf I in 1274, and in 1291 King Rudolf I granted it the privileges of a free imperial city.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-landau">Free Imperial City of Landau</a></li><li><blockquote>On 18 March 1793 the Rhenish-German National Convention proclaimed a free state covering the left bank of the Rhine from <b>Landau</b> to Bingen.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-mainz">Republic of Mainz</a></li></ul></section>
